# Merge into /etc/nut/upsmon.conf — one MONITOR line per UPS.
# NEVER commit real credentials; the passwords below are CHANGE_ME placeholders.
#
# MONITOR <ups>@<host> <powervalue> <user> <password> <primary|secondary>
#
#   <ups>@<host>  Device name from ups.conf, at the host running `upsd`. Use
#                 @localhost ONLY on the host physically cabled to the UPS. A
#                 host that watches over the network names the upsd host instead
#                 (e.g. ups1@eulerpi5). Getting this wrong = upsmon can't reach
#                 the UPS and every event silently no-ops.
#   <powervalue>  How many power feeds this UPS provides to THIS host. 1 = it
#                 actually powers this host and counts toward MINSUPPLIES; 0 =
#                 notification-only (watch it, but its battery state must never
#                 trigger this host's shutdown).
#   <user/pass>   The upsmon account from /etc/nut/upsd.users (needs
#                 `upsmon primary` or `upsmon secondary` there to match).
#   primary       This host runs the shutdown sequence and tells secondaries to
#                 power off first. Exactly one primary per UPS.
#   secondary     This host only observes and shuts itself down; it never drives
#                 others. Use on debug/observer hosts.
#
# Topology (eulerpi5): eulerpi5 owns the shutdown sequence, so its lines are
# `primary`. A separate rpi4 is USB-B cabled to the UPS(es) for debugging — on
# the rpi4 the same UPSes would be `secondary`. Confirm which host runs `upsd`
# before choosing @localhost vs @<host>: if the rpi4 is the USB/upsd server,
# eulerpi5 connects to ups@<rpi4>, not @localhost.

MONITOR ups1@localhost 0 upsmon CHANGE_ME primary
MONITOR ups2@localhost 0 upsmon CHANGE_ME primary
MONITOR ups3@localhost 0 upsmon CHANGE_ME primary

# ############################################################################
# MINSUPPLIES 0 IS DELIBERATE. Read this before raising it.
#
# MINSUPPLIES is how many powervalue units must remain before upsmon decides
# this host can no longer be powered and runs SHUTDOWNCMD. `forceshutdown()`
# is reachable ONLY when a MONITOR line gives this host a non-zero powervalue
# AND that total can fall below MINSUPPLIES. Setting MINSUPPLIES 0 with every
# powervalue at 0 makes it unreachable: upsmon still watches, still fires
# NOTIFYFLAGs, still drives the orchestrator — it just cannot power this box
# off on its own.
#
# That is the intended posture for the host running this orchestrator. It is
# the machine that has to SURVIVE an outage in order to bring the others back
# up afterwards, and to keep monitoring while they go down. Anything that can
# halt it defeats the point of running it.
#
# Note this is NOT covered by `shutdown.internal.enabled: false` in the
# orchestrator's own config. That flag governs the orchestrator's `local`
# shutdown targets only. upsmon's SHUTDOWNCMD is a separate authority living
# in THIS file, and nothing in the orchestrator can disarm it.
#
# An earlier revision of this snippet shipped two powervalue=1 feeds and
# MINSUPPLIES 1, which armed exactly that path. Installing from it converted
# the primary back into a machine that powers itself off, silently, while the
# operator-facing LOW BATTERY alert still said the shutdown policy decides.
#
# Raise this ONLY for a host that genuinely should power itself off when its
# feeds are gone — and then give the relevant MONITOR lines a powervalue to
# match. Verify afterwards:
#     grep -E '^MONITOR|^MINSUPPLIES' /etc/nut/upsmon.conf
# ############################################################################
MINSUPPLIES 0

# Route notifications through upssched (which calls the orchestrator). Every
# NOTIFYFLAG with EXEC must map to an event the orchestrator handles, and every
# handled event needs its flag here — otherwise config and handler drift apart.
NOTIFYCMD /sbin/upssched

# EXEC = run NOTIFYCMD; keep SYSLOG for journald visibility.
NOTIFYFLAG ONLINE   SYSLOG+EXEC
NOTIFYFLAG ONBATT   SYSLOG+EXEC
NOTIFYFLAG LOWBATT  SYSLOG+EXEC
NOTIFYFLAG COMMBAD  SYSLOG+EXEC
NOTIFYFLAG COMMOK   SYSLOG+EXEC

# NUT performs the real shutdown of THIS host when battery is critical. The
# orchestrator never runs this — it only observes and notifies.
SHUTDOWNCMD "/sbin/shutdown -h now"
